  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 331 AM CDT Tue Oct 6 2026\n\nThrough tonight: It will be dry through the period. The southern \nbranch of the jet stream will remain overhead but the latest surface \nanalysis had a >1020mb high centered over the southern Great lakes \nregion. This high will shift east through tonight but continue to \nridge back across our CWA from the northeast. Together these \nfeatures will result in dry weather but also continued warmer than \nnormal temperatures. /22/\n\nWednesday-Monday:\n\nThe National Hurricane Center is currently monitoring an area of \ndisorganized showers and storms associated with a trough over the \nfar southwestern Gulf (i.e. Invest 92L).  Global models are in \nagreement that this area of convection will become better organized \nlater this week, with NHC holding at an 80% chance of tropical \ncyclone development at this time. \n\nWPC has added an for \"Slight\" risk of excessive rainfall across the \nCWA associated with future tropical cyclone development. As a \nresult, an areawide \"Limited\" flooding HWO graphic will be issued \nthis forecast cycle. It's too early to determine exact rainfall \ntotals and impacts this potential system will have on the gulf coast \nor our forecast area. Residents and visitors to the region should \nmonitor the latest forecast for the most up-to-date information. \nOutside of tropical cyclone development, deep tropical moisture will \nbring rain chances ranging from 30-50% over the weekend. /19//SW/",
